      "credit": "DAMIT, Astronomical Institute of Charles University; J. Ďurech (2007), M. Delbo and P. Tanga (2009), Ďurech et al. (2011); model 197, version 2009-02-26.",
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0, DAMIT; retain original model and authors attribution.",
      "acquisition": "Restore the original counted triangle table and retain its source coordinates and connectivity through the pinned acquisition recipe.",
      "redistribution": "Original and derived model data with CC-BY-4.0 attribution; see NOTICE.md.",
      "lensId": "elevation",
      "projection": {
        "kind": "body-fixed-cartesian-triangular-mesh",
        "longitudeDirection": "east",
        "latitudeType": "planetocentric",
        "units": "Original archive coordinates flagged as km, with mesh volume-equivalent diameter 55.9999995 km and archive-declared diameter 56 km. The unchanged coordinates are uniformly rescaled by 0.87500000787523258 km per source unit to the explicitly selected published volume-equivalent diameter 49 km (Ďurech et al. (2011), Icarus 214, 652–670); the original archive diameter is not the adopted physical scale.",
        "metersPerUnit": 875.0000078752325,
        "referenceRadiusMeters": 24500
      },
      "coverage": "Convex light-curve reconstruction, 49 ± 5 km volume-equivalent diameter. The grid marks unavailable imagery; rotational phase is illustrative. The pole(79,−35) is preferred by both IRAS thermal fits and the2004 occultation negative chord. Delbo and Tanga2009 obtain a volume-equivalent thermal size range55–57 km, represented in DAMIT as56±1 km. The later Ďurech2011 Table3 fits the same pole family to five occultation chords at49±5 km. This explicit occultation volume scale is transferred uniformly to the archived shape; the distinct thermal estimate is retained as a comparison rather than silently averaged.",
